Found by k244 (ocp-infra-worker). The AMD side reads `status.allocatable` (k251/k252/rex-rancher k106) because the kubelet keeps `capacity` standing after a device plugin unregisters. The NVIDIA side still counts `nvidia.com/gpu` from capacity (ocp status nodes[].gpu and wherever else NVIDIA readiness is judged), so a node whose plugin died still reads as having its GPU.

Done when every NVIDIA GPU count/readiness check reads allocatable (capacity may be shown beside it, as the AMD section does), with tests for capacity 1 / allocatable 0. Lanes: ocp-status-worker (status), ocp-apply-worker if apply/node add judge readiness from capacity.

2026-10-31 02:38 Survey (ocp-status-worker): the only NVIDIA reader of status.capacity in lib/ is OCP::Kubernetes::node_gpu_count (and gpu_nodes through it); callers are OCP::Cmd::Status only (text GPU section + --json nodes[].gpu). OCP::Cmd::Node::Ls already reads allocatable. Apply side does NOT judge NVIDIA readiness from capacity: OCP::Cmd::Apply::Workloads::setup_gpu_operator (lib/OCP/Cmd/Apply/Workloads.pm ~449-469) and OCP::Cmd::Node::Add::_wait_gpu_vendors (lib/OCP/Cmd/Node/Add.pm ~778-794) detect NVIDIA nodes by NFD labels pci-0300_10de/pci-0302_10de; neither waits for nvidia.com/gpu at all (unlike AMDGPU.pm ~412-443, which waits for allocatable). No edit needed there; whether apply/node add should wait for nvidia.com/gpu allocatable as the AMD side does is a separate question for ocp-apply-worker. Robocop, Drift, Rex: no nvidia.com/gpu count.

2026-10-31 02:47 JSON decision: option 1 -- nodes[].gpu is now nvidia.com/gpu ALLOCATABLE (contract change, said in the Status.pm POD) and every node gains gpu_capacity (number, null when the node has none; always present, as ocp node ls --json keeps every key). Why: healthy nodes read the same as before, the two differ only where the plugin is gone/devices unhealthy -- exactly where the old gpu misled; and ocp node ls --json's gpu is already allocatable, so the two commands now agree. Text: healthy node prints the old line byte for byte ('otho: 1x NVIDIA GPU'); where capacity differs: 'otho: 0x NVIDIA GPU allocatable (capacity 1: stale after a device plugin unregisters, or unhealthy devices; only allocatable schedules)' -- the AMD line's reason, now one helper (_stale_gpu_capacity) both use. Per the card, capacity is shown only where it differs (AMD shows it always; left as is). gpu_nodes selects allocatable>0 OR capacity>0 so a plugin-gone node stays listed with its 0.

